\chapter{Compute Fabric \& Silicon Architecture}

\section{Multi-Core Processing Matrix}

The compute fabric integrates heterogeneous processing clusters composed of:
\begin{itemize}
    \item RISC-V scalar/vector tiles for deterministic low-latency control loops,
    \item Tensor-optimized accelerators for high-throughput simulation kernels,
    \item Cryptographic co-processors implementing lattice-based primitives.
\end{itemize}

Let the aggregate compute throughput be expressed as:
\[
\Phi_{\text{total}} =
\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{tiles}}}
\Big(
\Phi^{\text{scalar}}_i
+
\Phi^{\text{vector}}_i
+
\Phi^{\text{crypto}}_i
\Big),
\]
where each term corresponds to per-tile sustained GFLOP/s under thermal steady-state.

\section{High-Bandwidth Memory Fabric}

Memory channels are modeled using a multi-lane topology:
\[
B_{\text{mem}} = L \cdot W \cdot f_{\text{clk}},
\]
where $L$ is the number of lanes, $W$ the bus width, and $f_{\text{clk}}$ the effective clock rate.

A typical configuration:
\[
L = 8,\quad W = 128\text{-bit},\quad f_{\text{clk}} = 3.2\text{GHz},
\]
yielding:
\[
B_{\text{mem}} = 3.2768\ \text{TB/s}.
\]

